import{_ as e,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const q=JSON.parse('{"title":"Musician","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"generators/MUSICIAN.md","filePath":"generators/MUSICIAN.md"}'),n={name:"generators/MUSICIAN.md"};function r(h,t,d,i,u,l){return o(),a("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s('<h1 id="musician" tabindex="-1">Musician <a class="header-anchor" href="#musician" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Musician&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A pattern says which notes a track plays. A band does more with those notes than play them. A rhythm guitarist who hears the singer start a line plays softer, a lead guitarist waits for the singer to breathe before playing a lick, an organist holds a chord where a pianist strikes the chord and lets go, and the whole band plays a verse more gently than the chorus that follows. A song whose every track plays its pattern in full, all the time, sounds crowded and flat, however good the patterns are.</p><p>A Musician is one player of the band. The Musician is given a clip&#39;s notes and plays them as a player of its instrument would: differently in each kind of section, with room left for the tune, and with a touch of the player&#39;s own. Every note heard is a note the pattern wrote, which the Musician may move to the chord, leave out, hold, cut short, strike again or play louder or softer. The Musician writes no new part.</p><p>A musician reaches for a Musician when a song&#39;s tracks are right and the song still does not sound like a band. Make one Musician for each track, and add each to a chain on that track&#39;s clips.</p><h2 id="instrument" tabindex="-1">Instrument <a class="header-anchor" href="#instrument" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Instrument&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Instrument says who is playing, and with that what the player does unasked. The backing players, &quot;rhythm guitarist&quot;, &quot;backing piano&quot;, &quot;backing organ&quot;, &quot;backing synth&quot;, &quot;backup singers&quot;, &quot;horn section&quot; and &quot;string section&quot;, play softer while the tune sounds. The lead players, &quot;lead guitarist&quot;, &quot;lead organ&quot;, &quot;lead piano&quot;, &quot;lead synth&quot; and &quot;answering player&quot;, play only where the tune rests. The &quot;bassist&quot; gives way to nobody. An organ, a synth pad, singers and strings hold each note until the next, and a horn section plays short stabs. The &quot;drummer&quot; and the &quot;percussionist&quot; play drums, whose notes no chord moves.</p><p>Each instrument also has a lowest and a highest note, which Range respects.</p><h2 id="chords-from-and-tune-from" tabindex="-1">Chords From and Tune From <a class="header-anchor" href="#chords-from-and-tune-from" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Chords From and Tune From&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Chords from names the track whose chords the Musician follows, in the same scene, exactly as a <a href="./CHORDS">Chords</a> generator in Follow Progression mode does: the part is written once over chord I, and the Musician moves the notes to whatever chord that track plays. A Musician with a track in Chords from needs no Follow Chords in its chain.</p><p>Tune from names the track that carries the tune. The Musician listens to that track and makes room, as Gives way says.</p><h2 id="touch" tabindex="-1">Touch <a class="header-anchor" href="#touch" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Touch&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Activity says how much of the part the player plays. With &quot;light&quot; the player keeps the notes that start on a beat and every note a beat long or longer, and with &quot;sparse&quot; the notes on the first and third beats. With &quot;busy&quot; each note a quarter note or longer is struck twice, and with &quot;very busy&quot; each note an eighth note or longer.</p><p>Loudness moves every note louder or softer, from -3 to 3. Looseness, from 0 to 3, lets each note come out a little louder or softer than the last, as a hand does; 0 is a player who never wavers.</p><p>Gives way says what the player does while the tune sounds: &quot;none&quot;; &quot;some&quot;, softer; &quot;often&quot;, softer still and only the notes on the beats; or &quot;plays in the gaps&quot;, silent until the tune rests.</p><p>Accents says which notes of the bar the player leans on: &quot;downbeat&quot;, the first note of each bar; &quot;backbeat&quot;, beats two and four; or &quot;offbeats&quot;, the notes between the beats.</p><p>Notes says how the notes are held: &quot;as written&quot;, &quot;held&quot;, each until the next note starts, or &quot;short&quot;.</p><p>Range moves the part an octave down with &quot;low&quot; or up with &quot;high&quot;, and then brings any note the instrument cannot play back inside the instrument.</p><h2 id="kinds-of-section" tabindex="-1">Kinds of Section <a class="header-anchor" href="#kinds-of-section" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kinds of Section&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A scene can say what kind of section it is, on the Scene page: a verse, a chorus, a breakdown and so on. Under the Musician&#39;s settings is the Sections table, a row for each kind of section the song&#39;s scenes have, with two columns, Plays and Played. Left and Right move between them: Space switches Plays, and <code>[</code> and <code>]</code> step Played, or ENTER picks it from a list. Where the player sits out, Played shows <code>·</code>.</p><p>Plays says whether the player plays in that kind of section at all. A player who sits out the verses and comes in at the chorus is the oldest way a band makes a chorus feel bigger.</p><p>Played says how the part is played there:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Played</th><th>What is heard</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;whole&quot;</td><td>The part as the touch settings leave it</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;softer&quot;</td><td>Every note softer</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;thinned&quot;</td><td>Only the notes that start on a beat</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;head only&quot;</td><td>The first bar of every two</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;muted&quot;</td><td>Every note very short and a little softer, as a guitarist&#39;s palm mute</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;hits&quot;</td><td>Only the first note of each bar, a quarter note long at most</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="settings" tabindex="-1">Settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Settings&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Choices</th><th>A new Musician starts with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Name</td><td><code>ENTER</code> renames it, and every chain using it follows.</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Instrument</td><td>The fifteen players named above</td><td>&quot;rhythm guitarist&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Chords from</td><td>A track, or &quot;(none)&quot;</td><td>&quot;(none)&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Tune from</td><td>A track, or &quot;(none)&quot;</td><td>&quot;(none)&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Activity</td><td>&quot;sparse&quot;, &quot;light&quot;, &quot;as written&quot;, &quot;busy&quot;, &quot;very busy&quot;</td><td>&quot;as written&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Loudness</td><td>-3 to 3</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Looseness</td><td>0 to 3</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Gives way</td><td>&quot;none&quot;, &quot;some&quot;, &quot;often&quot;, &quot;plays in the gaps&quot;</td><td>What the instrument does</td></tr><tr><td>Accents</td><td>&quot;none&quot;, &quot;downbeat&quot;, &quot;backbeat&quot;, &quot;offbeats&quot;</td><td>&quot;none&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Notes</td><td>&quot;as written&quot;, &quot;held&quot;, &quot;short&quot;</td><td>What the instrument does</td></tr><tr><td>Range</td><td>&quot;low&quot;, &quot;as written&quot;, &quot;high&quot;</td><td>&quot;as written&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Plays, in a row of the Sections table</td><td>on or off</td><td>on</td></tr><tr><td>Played, in a row of the Sections table</td><td>&quot;whole&quot;, &quot;softer&quot;, &quot;thinned&quot;, &quot;head only&quot;, &quot;muted&quot;, &quot;hits&quot;</td><td>&quot;whole&quot;</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A drummer and a percussionist have no Chords from and no Range.</p><h2 id="applying-the-generator" tabindex="-1">Applying the Generator <a class="header-anchor" href="#applying-the-generator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Applying the Generator&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>examples/learning/24_musician.yml</code> plays a band&#39;s patterns as written and then hands the same patterns to Musicians, through a verse, a chorus and a breakdown, so that what each setting does is heard against the scene before.</p>',27)])])}const p=e(n,[["render",r]]);export{q as __pageData,p as default};
